This is a great set of things to prepare before your annual — I can help you organize talking points and questions. A few clarifying items first so I tailor this well:
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF)? If so, are you currently on any blood thinner (warfarin or a DOAC)?  
- Do you have any current joint or bone symptoms (hip/knee/shoulder pain, limited mobility, recent falls, difficulty with stairs) that make you think you need orthopedics?
- Quick med check: you listed levothyroxine 75 mcg; lisinopril 20 mg; atorvastatin 40 mg — what do you take for GERD? (helps me check interactions and causes)

Below are (A) what to ask and cover about AF management, (B) how to decide on an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics), and (C) a short checklist and items to bring to your PCP visit. If you want, I can turn these into an email or a script for the visit.

A. Atrial fibrillation — questions and points to raise
- Clarify diagnosis and symptoms
  - “Do I have atrial fibrillation or another arrhythmia on record? If so, when was it first diagnosed?”
  - “How often do episodes occur and what symptoms should prompt urgent care? (palpitations, chest pain, fainting, severe shortness of breath)”
- Stroke risk and anticoagulation
  - “Given my prior stroke and hypertension, what is my current stroke risk score (CHA2DS2‑VASc) and bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED)?”
  - “Should I be on an anticoagulant? If yes, would a DOAC (apixaban/rivaroxaban/dabigatran/edoxaban) be preferred over warfarin for me, and why?”
  - “Are there any lab/monitoring needs or drug interactions with my other meds (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin)?”
- Rhythm vs rate control and monitoring
  - “Do we need an ECG, echocardiogram, or ambulatory monitor (Holter/event monitor) to assess my heart rhythm or structure?”
  - “Should I see a cardiologist or electrophysiologist for consideration of rhythm control (medications, cardioversion, ablation)?”
- Modifiable contributors
  - “Could thyroid levels, sleep apnea, uncontrolled BP, or alcohol use be contributing? Should we check TSH, screen for sleep apnea, or adjust BP targets?”
- Medication safety and follow-up
  - “If anticoagulation is started, how often will I be followed and what bleeding precautions should I take?”
  - “If on warfarin, what INR target and testing schedule do you recommend?”

B. Deciding whether you need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Referral is usually based on symptoms and function rather than age alone. Ask:
  - “I’m 55 with prior stroke and HTN — do you recommend an orthopedics evaluation or would you first like to evaluate and order imaging (X‑ray, MRI) here?”
  - “I’m concerned about joint/bone health — do you recommend DEXA (bone density) screening given my age/risk factors?”
- When to refer to orthopedics (reasons that justify a direct referral)
  - Persistent, function-limiting joint pain despite conservative care (NSAIDs, PT, injections)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, or deformity
  - Imaging that shows severe osteoarthritis, fractures, or conditions that may need surgical assessment
  - Failure to improve after a trial of physical therapy or injections
- Alternatives to direct referral
  - Primary care can often order X‑rays, start PT, refer to physiatry (non‑operative), or trial conservative care and only refer to orthopedics if those measures fail.
- Specific question for Northwest Orthopedics (if you decide to refer)
  - “Based on my symptoms and any imaging you order, can you evaluate whether I’m a candidate for joint‑preserving procedures, injections, or joint replacement?”

C. What to bring and tests to consider at the annual
- Bring: complete med list (including OTCs/supplements), prior imaging or cardiology notes (echocardiogram, prior ECGs, any stroke hospital records), list of symptoms and functional limitations.
- Tests to discuss ordering:
  - ECG (baseline)
  - Basic labs: CBC, CMP, lipid panel, HbA1c (if indicated), TSH (especially since you’re on levothyroxine)
  - If AF or suspected: echocardiogram, ambulatory rhythm monitor
  - If anticoagulation considered: baseline renal function and liver tests (DOAC dosing depends on CrCl)
  - Bone health: DEXA if indicated (age 65 commonly screened for women; earlier if risk factors — ask PCP)
  - Imaging for joint pain: targeted X‑ray; MRI if soft tissue suspected
- Vaccines/other preventive care: influenza, COVID as recommended, shingles (if eligible), tetanus booster, colon cancer screening status

D. Safety red flags (get urgent care/ED if you experience)
- Sudden severe chest pain, fainting or near‑syncope, sudden severe shortness of breath, neurological symptoms (new weakness, slurred speech, sudden vision changes) — given stroke history, treat urgently.

