Atrial fibrillation — topics to cover with your PCP (and suggested wording)
- Diagnosis & history
  - “Do I currently have atrial fibrillation, or only intermittent episodes? When was the last documented episode?”
  - “Can you review my ECG/monitoring results with me?”

- Stroke risk and anticoagulation
  - “Given my history of stroke and hypertension, what is my current stroke risk score (CHA2DS2‑VASc) and what do you recommend about anticoagulation?”
  - “Should I be on an oral anticoagulant now (warfarin vs DOAC)? If yes, which one and why?”
  - “What bleeding risk assessment will you use (HAS‑BLED)? What precautions should I take?”

- Choice of anticoagulant and monitoring
  - “Do my current medications (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin, any GERD meds) interact with DOACs or warfarin?”
  - “If a DOAC is recommended, do I need renal function or liver tests before starting and periodically after?”
  - “If warfarin is chosen, how often will I need INR checks and target INR?”

- Rate vs rhythm control and symptom management
  - “Should we pursue rate-control (beta blockers/CCBs) or rhythm-control (antiarrhythmics, cardioversion, ablation) strategies for my situation?”
  - “Am I a candidate for catheter ablation or cardiology referral for rhythm control?”

- Additional testing and specialist referral
  - “Do you recommend an echocardiogram (to check valve/function), Holter monitor, or event monitor?”
  - “Would you like me to see a cardiologist/electrophysiologist? If so, can you refer me?”

- Lifestyle and comorbidities
  - “How do my blood pressure control, thyroid status (on levothyroxine), cholesterol, and sleep apnea risk affect AF management?”
  - “Any lifestyle changes (alcohol, caffeine, weight loss, sleep) you recommend to reduce AF episodes?”

Orthopedics referral — how to approach it and suggested questions
- Clarify the referral need
  - Before asking for a referral, be ready to describe symptoms: which joint(s), onset, pain severity, impact on daily activities, prior treatments (PT, injections, meds), and imaging (X‑ray/MRI).
  - Ask your PCP: “Given my age (55) and stroke history, do you think I should see Northwest Orthopedics for [hip/knee/shoulder/back] pain? What are the goals — diagnosis, conservative care, or surgical evaluation?”

- When a referral is reasonable
  - Consider referral if you have: limited function (difficulty walking, dressing, working), persistent moderate–severe pain despite conservative therapy (PT, NSAIDs, injections), mechanical symptoms (locking, giving way), abnormal or worsening imaging, or if you want an expert surgical opinion.
  - Also ask: “Are there specific pre-op evaluations or clearance considerations because of my stroke history and current medications (especially if I might need to stop anticoagulation for procedures)?”

Visit checklist (quick items to bring up or have ready)
- Confirm current med list and doses (bring a list or bottles).
- Bring recent peak flow (320) and any respiratory symptoms.
- Ask for these baseline labs/tests if not done recently: BMP/creatinine, CBC, LFTs, TSH (since you’re on levothyroxine), lipid panel, A1c if indicated, and ECG.
- If anticoagulation is being considered: renal function, liver tests, and urine/stool testing if bleeding risk concerns.
