I can help — but first: do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation, or are you wanting to raise screening/concern about AF with your PCP? That will change which questions are most important. Below I’ve put both (clear diagnosis vs. screening), plus guidance about an orthopedics referral and a ready-to-send draft you can use to message Dr. Nguyen. Tell me if you want me to draft or send this message.

If you already have AF (questions to bring to your annual physical or to a cardiology visit)
- Stroke prevention / anticoagulation
  - Given my prior stroke and hypertension, should I be on anticoagulation? (Ask specifically about CHA2DS2‑VASc and how your prior stroke changes recommendation.)
  - If anticoagulation is advised: what agent do you recommend (DOAC vs. warfarin)? What are pros/cons here given my kidney function, meds, and bleeding risk?
  - How should we assess bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED) and how to reduce modifiable bleeding risks?
- Rhythm vs. rate control
  - Is rhythm control (cardioversion, antiarrhythmics, ablation) or rate control preferred for me? What symptoms would push toward rhythm control?
  - If considering antiarrhythmic drugs, what monitoring and interactions are needed with my current meds (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin)?
- Monitoring and testing
  - Do I need an ECG now, ambulatory monitoring (Holter/event monitor), or echocardiogram?
  - How often should we check heart rate control and repeat monitoring?
- Medication interactions and safety
  - Any interactions between anticoagulants or antiarrhythmics and my current meds?
  - How will kidney and liver function affect anticoagulant choice/dosing? (Please check baseline creatinine/eGFR and LFTs.)
- Lifestyle and symptom management
  - Are there triggers to avoid (alcohol, stimulants, uncontrolled BP)?
  - When should I go to urgent care/ED (palpitations with chest pain, syncope)?
- Follow-up plan
  - When should I see cardiology? Would you refer me to cardiology/electrophysiology for AF management and stroke-prevention decisions?

If you do NOT have a formal AF diagnosis and want screening/questions to ask
- Symptoms and screening
  - Should we get an ECG today given my history of stroke and hypertension?
  - If my ECG is normal, would ambulatory monitoring be recommended if I have palpitations, lightheadedness, or transient symptoms?
- Risk assessment
  - Given prior stroke, do you recommend proactive cardiology evaluation even without known AF?
  - Any cardiac imaging (echo) or labs (thyroid function, electrolytes) you recommend as part of screening?
- Preventive measures
  - Are there BP targets or other risk factor changes you want to adjust because of my stroke history?

Specific tests and labs to ask for
- ECG
- Echocardiogram (to check cardiac structure, ejection fraction)
- Ambulatory monitor (24–48 hr Holter or event monitor) if symptoms or to look for asymptomatic AF
- Basic labs: BMP/creatinine (for DOAC dosing), LFTs, CBC (bleeding risk baseline), TSH (on levothyroxine)
- If anticoagulation started: baseline and periodic renal function monitoring; if warfarin considered — INR monitoring plan

About a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
