Assistant: Good — I can help you prepare. Below I’ll (1) summarize the most important points to discuss about atrial fibrillation (AF) management given your history, (2) list the tests and red flags to ask your PCP about, (3) give a clear set of questions to bring to your appointment (or to send to Dr. David Nguyen), (4) explain when an orthopedic referral to Northwest Orthopedics would be appropriate, and (5) offer a ready-to-use message you can send to your PCP asking for evaluation/referrals. Tell me if you want me to draft or send that message for you.

1) Key AF-related points given what you told me
- Your history of stroke is the most important factor here. If you have (or are found to have) AF, a prior stroke/ TIA gives a high stroke risk and generally makes anticoagulation strongly indicated.
- Calculate stroke risk (CHA2DS2‑VASc): you have hypertension (+1) and prior stroke (+2) = at least 3, which typically warrants anticoagulation if you have AF.
- Bleeding risk should also be assessed (HAS‑BLED), but a history of stroke usually does not rule out anticoagulation — it may increase the urgency of specialist review.
- Thyroid function matters: levothyroxine dose and TSH should be checked because hyperthyroidism (or over-replacement) can provoke AF.
- You should discuss rate vs rhythm control (medications, cardioversion, ablation) and stroke prevention (DOACs vs warfarin), and whether cardiology referral or monitoring (Holter, event monitor) is needed.
- Review current meds and interactions: lisinopril, atorvastatin, levothyroxine — generally not contraindications for DOACs, but renal function, liver function, and any other drugs matter.

2) Tests and evaluations to request before/at your annual physical
- 12‑lead ECG (to confirm AF or other arrhythmias)
- Ambulatory rhythm monitoring (Holter or event monitor) if intermittent symptoms are suspected
- Echocardiogram (to evaluate cardiac structure/function, left atrial size, valve disease)
- Labs: TSH, CBC, BMP (creatinine, electrolytes), LFTs, fasting glucose/HbA1c (if not recent), lipid panel (already on atorvastatin), and if already on warfarin — INR history
- Assessment of fall risk, bleeding history, and concomitant medications that raise bleed risk (NSAIDs, antiplatelets)
- If anticoagulation is started/changed: plan for follow‑up and patient education about signs of bleeding

3) Practical questions to ask your PCP (Dr. Nguyen) or cardiology
To PCP (first visit):
- “Can you check an ECG and TSH today and review my current meds in light of possible AF?”
- “Given my prior stroke and hypertension, should I be referred to cardiology for AF evaluation and stroke-prevention planning?”
- “Would you like to order an echocardiogram or ambulatory monitor?”
- “Do I need baseline labs (CBC, BMP, LFTs) before starting or changing anticoagulation?”
- “Do you recommend a cardiology referral now or only if AF is confirmed on ECG/monitoring?”

To cardiology (if referred):
- “Based on my prior stroke and HTN, do you recommend starting anticoagulation now or after further testing?”
- “Which anticoagulant (DOAC vs warfarin) do you recommend for me and why?”
- “Should I pursue rhythm control (antiarrhythmic meds, cardioversion, ablation) or rate control?”
- “What monitoring do you recommend (Holter, event monitor, implantable loop recorder)?”
- “How should we manage my levothyroxine dose and monitor TSH in relation to AF?”

4) When to get a Northwest Orthopedics referral
Age alone is not usually a reason to refer. Consider an ortho referral if you have:
- Persistent joint pain > 3 months that limits activities (walking, climbing stairs, work)
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching, instability), suspected rotator cuff tears, or prior injury that hasn’t improved
- Significant functional decline (difficulty dressing, getting in/out of car, climbing stairs)
- Imaging showing advanced osteoarthritis, fracture, or structural problem that may require surgery
- Failure of conservative measures (PT, weight loss, bracing, steroid injections) and ongoing severe symptoms
- Recurrent falls related to joint instability or pain (this is especially important given anticoagulation considerations)

If you don’t have significant pain or functional limitations, PCP management (PT, meds, injections, imaging) is reasonable first-line. If you do have the issues above, asking for a referral to Northwest Orthopedics is appropriate.

5) Red flags — bring these up urgently
- New neurological symptoms (weakness, slurred speech, sudden severe headache) — stroke emergency
- New dizziness, fainting, or syncope
- New or worsening shortness of breath, chest pain
- Recurrent falls while on or being considered for anticoagulation

6) Suggested brief message you can send to Dr. Nguyen
(If you want, I can draft this and either save as a draft or send it for you. Tell me whether to send or draft, and any extra details you want included.)

Suggested message you can copy/paste:
