Good — I can help you prepare. Below I’ll (A) list the key AF-related questions and tests to bring up with Dr. Nguyen, (B) list when an orthopedic referral is reasonable and what to ask Northwest Orthopedics, (C) flag a few medication / testing details to confirm, and (D) offer an email template you can send or save as a draft to your PCP. Tell me if you want me to draft or send the email.

A. Questions and topics to cover about atrial fibrillation (AF) management
- Confirm diagnosis and type
  - Do I have persistent, paroxysmal, or permanent AF? When was it diagnosed?
- Stroke prevention
  - What is my current stroke-risk score (CHA2DS2‑VASc) and how does that affect whether I should be on anticoagulation?
  - Given my prior stroke, do you recommend anticoagulation now (DOAC vs warfarin)? If DOAC, which one and why?
  - What is my bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED) and how should that influence choice or monitoring of anticoagulation?
- Current anticoagulation status and management
  - Am I already on an anticoagulant? If not, what are the risks/benefits of starting one?
  - If I need surgery or procedures, how will anticoagulation be managed or bridged?
- Rhythm vs rate control and symptoms
  - Should we try rhythm control (antiarrhythmic meds, cardioversion, ablation) or focus on rate control?
  - Would you refer me to an electrophysiologist for consideration of ablation? What results/risks should I expect given my stroke history?
- Diagnostic and monitoring tests
  - Do I need an ECG, echocardiogram, Holter/event monitor, or longer rhythm monitoring (e.g., implantable loop recorder)?
  - How frequently should monitoring occur?
- Contributing factors and optimization
  - Check thyroid function (TSH) — is my levothyroxine dose appropriate? (Thyroid status can trigger AF.)
  - Check blood pressure control, electrolytes, kidney function (creatinine/GFR) — important for anticoagulant dosing.
  - Any interactions between my current meds (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin — please confirm doses) and anticoagulants?
- Follow-up plan
  - How often should I have labs or visits if on anticoagulation? When should I seek urgent care for AF symptoms?

B. Orthopedics — when a referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense and what to ask
I’m missing specifics about your orthopedic concern (which joint, pain level, activity limits). If you have any of the following, a referral is reasonable:
- Persistent or worsening joint pain despite conservative care (PT, NSAIDs, steroid injection)
- Functional limitations interfering with daily activities or work
- Suspected structural damage (instability, locking, mechanical symptoms)
- Prior imaging showing advanced arthritis, meniscal tear, rotator cuff tear, etc.
- Concern for surgery (joint replacement, arthroscopy)
What to ask Northwest Orthopedics (or ask your PCP to refer with these questions)
- Based on my symptoms and imaging, would you recommend conservative care vs surgical evaluation?
- What diagnostics do you need (X-ray, MRI)? I can bring previous images/reports.
- If surgery is recommended: how will my anticoagulation and stroke history be managed (need cardiology clearance)?
- What are expected recovery time, risks, and alternatives (PT, injections, bracing)?
Other considerations given your medical history
- Because of prior stroke and possible anticoagulation, ask about perioperative anticoagulation management and bleeding risk.
- Consider bone health screening (DEXA) if there are fracture risk concerns.

C. Clarifications and tests I recommend you confirm before the visit
- Please confirm your current meds and doses (you listed levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg — you also mentioned GERD but didn’t list a PPI). I can’t be precise about interactions until meds are confirmed.
- Do you have a known diagnosis of atrial fibrillation now, or are you just concerned about AF risk?
- Are you currently on an anticoagulant or antiplatelet (e.g., aspirin, clopidogrel)?
- Any recent labs (kidney function, INR if on warfarin), recent ECG, or recent echocardiogram?
- Any history of major bleeding or GI bleeding? Any falls?
- Sex (male/female) and whether you are on any hormone therapy — needed for precise CHA2DS2‑VASc.

Tests to consider ordering (discuss with PCP)
- ECG and possibly echocardiogram
- Holter or event monitor if intermittent palpitations/syncope
- TSH (thyroid function)
- Basic metabolic panel (including creatinine), CBC, lipid panel, A1c (if not recent)
- DEXA if concerned about bone health
- Peak flow/spirometry if pulmonary symptoms (you mentioned prior peak flow 320)

D. Email template you can send to Dr. David Nguyen (Central Austin Primary Care)
