Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you prepare focused questions and a short plan to take to your annual physical with Dr. David Nguyen. Below I’ll (A) list targeted questions to ask about atrial fibrillation (AF) management, (B) outline what tests you might request beforehand or at the visit, (C) cover anticoagulation considerations given your stroke history, and (D) give a checklist and suggested questions about whether a referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense. Tell me if you want me to draft an email to Dr. Nguyen asking to address these at the visit.

A — Key AF-related questions to bring up
- Do I have (or am I at high risk for) atrial fibrillation? If not diagnosed, should we screen (ECG, ambulatory monitor) because of my stroke history?
- If I have AF, do we recommend rate control or rhythm control for me? What symptoms or test results would change that decision?
- Given my prior stroke, do you recommend anticoagulation? If so, which anticoagulant is best for me (DOAC vs warfarin) and why?
- What is my CHA2DS2-VASc score and how does that affect anticoagulation choice? (Note: with hypertension + prior stroke/TIA, that score is at least 3 — usually anticoagulation is recommended, but confirm with you.)
- What are the bleeding risks for anticoagulation in my case (HAS-BLED or other considerations)? How would a prior stroke affect bleeding risk or choice of agent?
- How will my current medications (lisinopril, atorvastatin, levothyroxine) affect anticoagulant choice or dosing? Any interactions to worry about?
- What monitoring will be needed if I start anticoagulation (kidney function, liver tests, INR if warfarin)? How often?
- Do I need an echocardiogram, Holter/event monitor, or other cardiac testing (e.g., stress test) before deciding on long-term management?
- Should my thyroid level (TSH) be checked because thyroid disease can trigger or worsen AF?
- What symptoms should prompt urgent care or ER (rapid heart rate, syncope, chest pain, worsening shortness of breath)?
- If rhythm control or procedures (cardioversion, ablation) are considered, what are the expected benefits/risks for someone with my history (age 55, prior stroke, HTN)?

B — Tests to consider ordering before/or at the visit
- 12-lead ECG (to check for AF)
- Ambulatory rhythm monitoring (Holter or event monitor) if intermittent palpitations or suspected paroxysmal AF
- Transthoracic echocardiogram (evaluate heart structure/function)
- Basic labs: CMP (kidney/liver function), CBC, TSH (given levothyroxine), lipid panel, HbA1c (cardiac risk), and baseline renal function for DOAC dosing (eGFR)
- If already on anticoagulation: INR if warfarin
- Carotid duplex or neurovascular follow-up if recommended around prior stroke (ask if last imaging is available)
- Consider baseline orthopedics imaging only if joint symptoms present (see below)

C — Anticoagulation & stroke risk — things to raise explicitly
- With prior stroke you likely meet threshold where anticoagulation is strongly considered if AF is present. Ask which agent the PCP recommends and why.
- If anticoagulation is started, ask about:
  - Kidney dosing requirements (DOAC dose adjustments)
  - Drug interactions (warfarin interactions more common — levothyroxine can influence dosing; many drugs affect DOAC levels)
  - Fall risk and how to mitigate it (physical therapy, home safety, review of blood pressure control)
  - Peri-procedure planning (if you need surgery/orthopedics later)
- Confirm who will coordinate anticoagulation monitoring (PCP vs cardiology) and whether you should see a cardiologist/electrophysiologist.

D — Orthopedics referral — when it’s appropriate and what to ask
If you’re wondering whether to request a referral to Northwest Orthopedics, consider these points and questions:
- When to refer:
  - You have persistent or progressive joint pain limiting daily activities despite conservative measures (NSAIDs as tolerated, physical therapy, rest)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving way), instability, or visible deformity
  - Need for imaging (X-ray/MRI) that your PCP can’t or won’t order without a specialist
  - You’re considering injections or surgery
- Questions to ask Dr. Nguyen before referring:
  - Do my mobility symptoms warrant imaging or conservative care (PT, injections) first?
  - Are there risk factors (anticoagulation, prior stroke) that change the timing or type of orthopedic interventions?
  - If surgery is possible, how will anticoagulation be managed pre-/post-op? Who coordinates that?
  - Do you recommend bone density testing (DEXA) given age 55 and stroke history/fall risk?
- If you do see Northwest Orthopedics, ask them:
  - What conservative treatments should I try first and for how long?
  - If surgery is an option, what are the specific risks related to my stroke history and anticoagulation?
  - What anesthesia and perioperative plans do you use for patients on anticoagulants?

E — Practical visit checklist (what to bring/prepare)
- Up-to-date medication list (doses and why you take them) — include levothyroxine, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg, and any OTC meds/supplements
- Record of prior stroke (date, cause, hospitalization notes) and any imaging if available
- Note any heart symptoms (palpitations, skipped beats, syncope, dizziness, chest pain, worsening shortness of breath)
- Your last peak flow (320) and any breathing symptoms — mention if you want pulmonary follow-up or spirometry
- A list of functional limitations or specific orthopedic complaints (location, duration, things that worsen/improve it)
- Ask the clinic to order the ECG and the labs listed above before/during the visit if possible

F — Offer/help
- I can draft a concise email or message to Dr. Nguyen requesting evaluation of possible AF and asking whether he recommends anticoagulation and an orthopedics referral. Would you like me to draft and (a) save as a draft, or (b) send it now? If yes, tell me whether you want to include any specific personal symptoms or the date/details of your prior stroke.

If you want, I’ll also create a one-page printout of these questions you can bring to your appointment or a short patient-facing script to read during the visit. Which would you prefer?

