A. Questions to ask about atrial fibrillation and stroke prevention
- Confirm the basics
  - Have I ever had an official diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (paroxysmal, persistent, or permanent) documented in my chart?
  - When was the last ECG, Holter, or cardiac rhythm monitor? Can we review the results?
- Stroke risk and anticoagulation
  - Based on my history (age 55, hypertension, prior stroke), how do you assess my stroke risk (CHA2DS2‑VASc)? Would you recommend anticoagulation?
  - If anticoagulation is recommended: what options (warfarin vs DOACs) would you consider best for me and why?
  - Are there any contraindications for anticoagulation in my case (e.g., prior hemorrhagic stroke, recent major bleeding)? (If your prior stroke was hemorrhagic — ask specifically.)
- Bleeding risk and monitoring
  - How would you assess my bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED or other)? What precautions would you recommend?
  - What baseline labs do we need before starting or changing anticoagulation? (e.g., CBC, creatinine, liver function)
- Medication interactions and thyroid
  - Could my current medications (lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg, levothyroxine 75 mcg) affect anticoagulant choice or dosing?
  - Could my thyroid status (on levothyroxine) be contributing to palpitations or AF risk? When was my last TSH?
- Rate vs rhythm strategy and symptoms
  - If I have AF, should we pursue rate control or rhythm control? What symptoms/findings would push toward cardiology referral or ablation?
  - Do I need a cardiology or electrophysiology referral now for management options (ablation, cardioversion)?
- Testing and follow-up
  - Do you recommend an echocardiogram or stress test?
  - How often should rhythm be monitored (ECG, event monitor, or implantable loop recorder)?
  - If anticoagulation is started, how will follow-up and monitoring be handled (lab checks, dosing changes, drug interactions)?

B. Questions about whether you need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Start with symptom-based screening
  - What specific joint or musculoskeletal problem are you concerned about? (e.g., knee pain, hip pain, shoulder, back, loss of function)
  - Is the issue causing functional limitations (walking, stairs, work) or significant pain despite conservative measures?
- Conservative management first
  - Have I tried appropriate conservative therapy (physical therapy, exercise program, weight management, NSAIDs when appropriate, steroid injections)? How long should these be tried before referral?
  - Do you recommend imaging first (X‑ray, MRI) before a referral?
- When to refer to orthopedics
  - At what point would you send me to Northwest Orthopedics for surgical evaluation (persistent pain > months, failed PT, progressive disability, deformity, recurrent instability)?
  - Given my history of stroke and any anticoagulation, what pre‑op evaluations or precautions would be needed if surgery were considered?
- Practical referral questions
  - If you think a referral is appropriate, can you send it to Northwest Orthopedics? Any preferred surgeon there for my concern?
  - Should I have pre‑referral studies (imaging, bone density, labs) done first?
  - If surgery is a possibility, how will anticoagulation be managed peri‑operatively?

C. What to bring / have ready for the appointment
- Up‑to‑date medication list (include doses and reasons: levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg, any antiplatelets/anticoagulants)
- Dates/details of prior stroke (date, ischemic vs hemorrhagic, any residual effects)
- Any recent ECGs, Holter reports, echocardiogram, or cardiology notes
- Recent labs (TSH, creatinine/eGFR, CBC, liver tests) if available
- Description of orthopedic symptoms: onset, severity, things that help/worsen, prior treatments, imaging you have
- Your peak flow value (you noted 320) and any pulmonary history if relevant
- Questions written down so you don’t forget them

D. Quick clinical context (for discussion with Dr. Nguyen)
- A prior stroke gives a high stroke-risk weight in anticoagulation decisions (CHA2DS2‑VASc gives 2 points for previous stroke/TIA), so anticoagulation is commonly recommended if a patient has AF — but the type of prior stroke (ischemic vs hemorrhagic) matters a lot. Make sure to clarify that detail with your PCP.
- Thyroid dysfunction (over- or under-replacement) can affect heart rhythm — ask about TSH checks.
- If an orthopedic procedure is being considered, coordination between cardiology, your PCP, and orthopedics is important for safe perioperative anticoagulation and stroke-risk management.

Next step
