Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good start for preparing for your visit. A few quick clarifying questions that will help me tailor the suggestions:
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF), or are you asking because of symptoms/concern? If yes, is it paroxysmal, persistent, or permanent?  
- Are you currently taking any anticoagulant (warfarin or a DOAC)?  
- What sex are you (male/female)? (CHA2DS2-VASc uses female sex as one point.)  
- When was your stroke? Any residual deficits or recent neurology follow-up?  
- Can you confirm your current meds (you listed levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg — levothyroxine is for thyroid replacement; just want to be sure there aren’t other meds like a PPI for GERD or an anticoagulant)?  
- Is your joint/orthopedic concern around a particular joint or symptom (pain, instability, limited range of motion, falls)?

Below are focused question lists and things to raise with Dr. Nguyen (and with cardiology/orthopedics as appropriate).

A. Questions to ask about atrial fibrillation management
(Use these at the visit or ask for cardiology referral if you don’t already have one)

Diagnosis and monitoring
- Do I have AF (or is this preventive/screening)? If AF is present, what type (paroxysmal, persistent, permanent)?  
- Do I need an ECG now? A 24–48 hour Holter or event monitor to look for paroxysms? How often should I be monitored?

Stroke prevention / anticoagulation
- Based on my history (hypertension and prior stroke), do I need anticoagulation? (Note: prior stroke/TIA gives a high stroke-risk score and usually indicates anticoagulation.)  
- Which anticoagulant do you recommend — warfarin vs a DOAC — and why? What are the pros/cons for me?  
- If a DOAC is recommended, do we need baseline renal function (eGFR) and how will dosing be adjusted?  
- If warfarin is considered, how often will INR be checked and what is the target range?  
- What are my bleeding risks and signs to watch for? Should we calculate a HAS-BLED score?  
- If I start anticoagulation, how will that affect other procedures (dental, potential orthopedic surgery)?

Rate vs rhythm control and symptoms
- If I have AF, should we pursue rate control (meds like beta-blockers or calcium-channel blockers) or rhythm control (antiarrhythmic meds, cardioversion, ablation)? What do you recommend based on my stroke history and symptoms?  
- What symptoms should prompt urgent care (lightheadedness, syncope, chest pain, severe shortness of breath)?

Further testing and referrals
- Do I need an echocardiogram (to check ejection fraction, valvular disease) or carotid/brain imaging given my stroke history?  
