This is a good set of concerns to get organized before your annual — below I’ll (1) clarify a few details I’d want you to confirm, (2) give focused questions to ask about atrial fibrillation management, (3) give focused questions and criteria for an orthopedics referral, (4) list tests/records to bring or request, and (5) provide a short email you can send to Dr. David Nguyen asking for guidance (I can draft/send it if you want).

1) Quick clarifying checks for accuracy
- You listed levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, and atorvastatin 40 mg — levothyroxine is for thyroid replacement, lisinopril is for blood pressure, atorvastatin for cholesterol. Please confirm which meds are for which condition and any other meds (especially anticoagulants, antiplatelets, or OTCs like aspirin, NSAIDs) and allergies.  
- Confirm your sex (CHA2DS2-VASc includes female sex as a point).  
- Confirm whether you currently have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF) or whether you’re concerned about it (and if so, any symptoms: palpitations, syncope, lightheadedness, SOB, chest pain).  
- Confirm the date/meaning of the “last peak flow 320” (predicted values vary by height/sex/age) and whether you have lung disease symptoms.

2) Questions to ask about atrial fibrillation (AF) management
- Do I currently have AF (paroxysmal, persistent, permanent)? If so, when was it first diagnosed and what triggered it?  
- Given my age (55), history of hypertension, and prior stroke, what is my estimated stroke risk (CHA2DS2‑VASc) and do you recommend anticoagulation? (Note: with prior stroke + hypertension the score is usually ≥3 which typically indicates anticoagulation is recommended — please confirm my exact score.)  
- If anticoagulation is recommended, what agent do you prefer (DOAC vs warfarin) and why? What tests (kidney function, liver function) do you need before starting? How will bleeding risk be assessed/managed?  
- If I’m already on an anticoagulant/antiplatelet, how should we manage it around procedures or if I need surgery (e.g., orthopedic surgery)?  
- Should I be referred to cardiology or electrophysiology for rhythm management (cardio version, ablation) or for complex anticoagulation decisions?  
- What monitoring do you recommend? (ECG now, ambulatory monitor/Holter/event monitor, echocardiogram, thyroid function tests — since thyroid dysfunction can trigger AF.)  
- Are there medication interactions to worry about with my current meds (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin) or OTCs I take?  
- Should I be evaluated for sleep apnea or other reversible contributors to AF?  
- If I’m symptomatic, what are the options for rate vs rhythm control and what are the pros/cons for me specifically?

3) Questions/criteria about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or orthopedics in general)
- What is the specific orthopedic problem/complaint? (e.g., joint pain limiting activities, progressive disability, failed conservative therapy) — if you tell me your symptoms I can tailor this.  
- General referral considerations:
  - Refer if you have persistent, function-limiting joint pain despite conservative care (NSAIDs if safe, physical therapy, injections) OR imaging showing advanced osteoarthritis affecting function.  
  - Refer if there is mechanical instability, deformity, or neurologic compromise (e.g., radicular symptoms), or if you’re considering surgical options (joint replacement, arthroscopy).  
  - If you’re on an anticoagulant (or likely to need one), coordinate with PCP/cardiology about perioperative anticoagulation management before orthopedic procedures.  
- Questions to ask orthopedics if referred: likely diagnosis, expected benefit from surgery vs continued conservative care, risks given my stroke history and any anticoagulation, pre-op optimization needed (cardiac clearance, blood thinner plan), rehab expectations and timeline.

4) Tests and documentation to bring/request at your annual visit
- Recent ECG (12‑lead) and any prior ECGs/Holter/event monitor reports.  
- Labs: basic metabolic panel (to check kidney function — important for DOAC dosing), CBC, liver function tests, TSH (given levothyroxine), lipids, A1c if diabetic risk.  
- Echo if you’ve ever had one (to assess cardiac structure/function).  
- Medication list (prescription, OTCs, herbals), vaccination status, and details about prior stroke (when, type, residual deficits).  
- Imaging of the painful joint(s) if you are considering orthopedics (X‑rays, MRI if available).  
- Falls history and functional status (important for surgical risk and anticoagulation decisions).

5) Suggested short email you can send to Dr. Nguyen
Subject: Annual visit — review AFib/stroke risk and possible orthopedics referral

Hi Dr. Nguyen — I’m preparing for my annual and want to review two issues ahead of time.

1) Atrial fibrillation/stroke prevention — I have HTN and a prior stroke. Please review whether I need anticoagulation (and what tests you’d like done first — e.g., BMP/creatinine, LFTs, TSH, ECG/echo/Holter), and whether you recommend cardiology referral for rhythm management.

2) Orthopedics referral — I’d like your opinion on whether I should see Northwest Orthopedics for [brief symptom summary, e.g., "right knee pain limiting walking for 6 months, tried PT and NSAIDs"] and any pre-referral tests or precautions you want.

Current meds: levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg. Please let me know what labs/imaging to get before the visit or if you’d like me to schedule a televisit first.

Thanks,  
[Your name]

