Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good plan — I’ll help you organize the key points and specific questions to bring to your annual with Dr. Nguyen so you can cover atrial fibrillation (AF) management and whether an orthopedics referral is needed.

First — a quick clarification to bring up at the visit
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation, or are you concerned about symptoms (palpitations, lightheadedness, unexplained fatigue, syncope)? If AF is not diagnosed, some of the tests below will be aimed at screening/confirmation.

Section A — AFib management: items to discuss and specific questions
Why it matters for you: with hypertension and a prior stroke, stroke-prevention decisions are particularly important.

Key topics and questions
- Stroke-risk and anticoagulation
  - “Given my history of stroke and hypertension, should I be on anticoagulation to prevent another stroke?”
  - “Can you explain my CHA2DS2‑VASc score and whether anticoagulation is recommended for me?”
  - “If anticoagulation is recommended, what options (DOAC vs warfarin) are best given my medications and kidney function?”
  - “If I go on anticoagulation, how do we manage interactions with levothyroxine, lisinopril, and atorvastatin?”

- Bleeding risk and monitoring
  - “What is my bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED or similar) and how would we minimize it?”
  - “What baseline labs (CBC, renal function, liver tests) do you want before starting or changing anticoagulation?”

- Rhythm vs rate control and symptoms
  - “If I have AF, should we pursue rate control (beta-blocker/CCB) or rhythm control (antiarrhythmic, cardioversion, ablation) for my situation?”
  - “What symptoms or test results would prompt a referral to cardiology or an electrophysiologist?”

- Diagnostic testing / monitoring
  - “Should I have an ECG today? If AF is suspected intermittently, should I get a Holter monitor or event monitor?”
  - “Do you recommend an echocardiogram to check heart structure/function?”
  - “How often should AF be rechecked once we start a treatment plan?”

- Coordination of care
  - “Do you recommend a cardiology/referral to an electrophysiologist now, or only if tests show AF or I remain symptomatic?”
  - “If I need a procedure (e.g., ablation, cardioversion), what pre-procedure cardiac clearance and medication adjustments will be required?”

Section B — Orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics): when it’s appropriate
You didn’t describe joint or orthopedic symptoms here — use the PCP visit to decide. Typical reasons to refer:
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness > 3 months despite conservative treatment (NSAIDs, PT, injections)
- Significant loss of function or ADLs (walking, stairs, dressing)
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching, instability), deformity or suspected advanced osteoarthritis
- Prior injury or imaging that suggests need for surgical evaluation

Questions to ask Dr. Nguyen about an orthopedics referral
- “Based on my age and medical history, do you recommend a referral to Northwest Orthopedics for my [hip/knee/shoulder/back] pain?”
- “What conservative measures should be tried first (physical therapy, injections, meds), and how long should I try them before seeing ortho?”
- “If I need surgery, how would my stroke history and current meds affect surgical risk and perioperative anticoagulation management?”
- “Do you want pre‑referral imaging (X‑ray, MRI) or labs before I see ortho?”

Pre-op / coordination concerns (if surgery is a possibility)
- Discuss anticoagulation plan (who stops/bridges meds and timing)
- Need for cardiac clearance given prior stroke — ask whether cardiology clearance is required pre-op
- DVT prophylaxis strategies and perioperative blood pressure control

Section C — Tests and items to bring up at your annual
- Cardiac-focused:
  - ECG (12‑lead)
  - Consider echocardiogram if AF or structural concerns
  - Ambulatory monitor (Holter/event) if intermittent palpitations
  - Baseline labs: BMP (kidney function), CBC, LFTs, coagulation profile if on warfarin
  - TSH (you’re on levothyroxine; check dose adequacy)
- Routine annual items:
  - Blood pressure check, lipid panel, A1c (if diabetes risk), vaccination review (flu, shingles, etc.)
  - Peak flow today (you reported 320 previously) and respiratory symptom review
- Medication review:
  - Bring a current med list with doses (levothyroxine 75 mcg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg)
  - Bring list of allergies and any OTC supplements (esp. OTC NSAIDs, aspirin)

Section D — Practical phrasing you can use in the visit
- “I have hypertension and a prior stroke. Should we screen for atrial fibrillation today, and would you recommend anticoagulation if AF is found?”
- “If I needed an orthopedics consult or possible surgery, what would you advise given my cardiac history?”
- “Can you order an ECG and basic labs today and advise whether I should see cardiology or orthopedics next?”

Safety note and next step
- I’m not a clinician; this isn’t medical advice. These points are to help you have an efficient, informed conversation with Dr. Nguyen. Because of your prior stroke, AF-related stroke prevention and medication coordination are high-priority topics to cover at the visit.

Would you like:
- A one-page checklist you can print/take to the appointment (I can draft that)? 
- Or a short email draft to Dr. Nguyen summarizing your concerns and asking for specific tests/referrals?

