  - When to get cardiology referral:
    - New diagnosis of AF, recurrent symptomatic AF despite initial therapy, complex anticoagulation questions, consideration of ablation or left atrial appendage closure, or unexplained embolic stroke — request cardiology if any of those apply.

Orthopedics — when a referral makes sense
- Age 55 alone is not an automatic reason to see orthopedics. Reasonable indications to refer:
  - Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily activities despite conservative care (physical therapy, weight loss, NSAIDs)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, or recurrent falls
  - X‑ray/MRI showing advanced osteoarthritis, torn meniscus, rotator cuff tear, significant degenerative changes that might benefit from surgery
  - Prior fractures or hardware issues needing specialist input
  - You want a surgical opinion for joint replacement or other procedures
