Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get organized before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions first so I tailor the list:
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF)? If so, are you currently on an anticoagulant (warfarin or a DOAC such as apixaban/rivaroxaban)?  
- Do you have current cardiology follow-up (electrophysiologist or cardiologist)?  
- Can you confirm your full current med list and doses (I want to be sure I interpret what you wrote about levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin, and any GERD meds)?  
- Any bleeding problems, prior GI bleeds, or recent surgeries?

Below are two focused sections you can use at your visit (or send to Dr. Nguyen) — questions about AF management, and questions to decide on an orthopedics referral to Northwest Orthopedics — plus practical tests and items to bring.

A. Questions to ask about atrial fibrillation management
- Do I have AF (or is it suspected)? If yes:
  - What is my current stroke risk score (CHA2DS2-VASc) and what does that mean for anticoagulation? (Note: prior stroke gives 2 points and usually means anticoagulation is recommended unless contraindicated.)
  - Am I currently on anticoagulation? If not, should I start one now? If yes, is it the best choice (warfarin vs DOAC)? Why this one for me?
  - What is my bleeding risk (HAS-BLED) and how should we manage modifiable bleeding risks?
  - Do I need an echocardiogram (transthoracic echo) to evaluate heart structure/function or a transesophageal echo before cardioversion or other procedures?
  - Should I get ambulatory rhythm monitoring (24–48 hr Holter or 2–4 week event monitor/implantable loop recorder) to quantify AF burden/symptoms?
  - Rate vs rhythm strategy: Based on my symptoms and stroke history, should we aim to control rate only or try rhythm control (meds or ablation)?
  - Would a referral to an electrophysiologist (EP) be appropriate for consideration of catheter ablation or more advanced options?
  - If anticoagulation is recommended: which labs do you need before starting (CBC, BMP/creatinine, LFTs, TSH) and how often should we monitor them?
  - If warfarin is being considered: what INR target, and how will dosing be managed? If a DOAC: do we need a baseline creatinine/GFR for dosing?
  - How does my levothyroxine (and thyroid function) affect AF risk? Should we check TSH/free T4 now? (Over- or under-replacement can provoke arrhythmia.)
  - If cardioversion or a procedure is needed: what is the plan for peri‑procedural anticoagulation?
  - When should I go to the ER? (e.g., syncope, chest pain, severe shortness of breath, very fast uncontrolled rate)

B. Tests and labs to request / bring
- Recent ECG (12-lead) and any prior cardiology notes/echo reports
- Ambulatory rhythm monitor (if symptomatic or AF suspected)
- Echocardiogram (if not done recently)
- Labs: CBC, BMP/creatinine/eGFR, LFTs, TSH, fasting lipid panel, HbA1c (if diabetic risk), coagulation/INR if on warfarin
- If on DOAC: baseline creatinine/renal function for dosing
- Carotid imaging or neurology follow-up if stroke history has unresolved questions (ask PCP if that’s current)
- Medication list (all OTC and supplements) and any recent bleeding events

C. Medication and interaction points to discuss
- Anticoagulant options and how your other meds (atorvastatin, lisinopril, levothyroxine, any GERD meds) might interact — and whether dosages need adjustment for kidney function.
- Thyroid control: ensure TSH is in target range to reduce AF risk.
- Blood pressure control: confirm target BP and adherence to lisinopril.
- Lipid management: atorvastatin dose and goals given stroke history.

D. When to refer to cardiology / electrophysiology
- New diagnosis of AF, recurrent symptomatic AF, AF with prior stroke, unclear anticoagulation strategy, or if you’re being considered for cardioversion or ablation — these are reasons to refer to cardiology/EP.

E. Thinking through a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
I can help decide whether you need an ortho referral. Useful info to share with your PCP or bring to the appointment:
- What are your symptoms? (location, severity, duration, mechanical symptoms like catching/locking, functional limitations)
- Have you tried conservative treatment? (physical therapy, NSAIDs, activity modification, injections)
- Does pain limit daily activities or work, or cause falls/instability?
- Any imaging already (X-ray, MRI)? Bring copies.
- Any prior joint surgeries or major injuries?
Why a referral may be appropriate now:
- Persistent pain >6–8 weeks despite conservative care
- Mechanical/instability symptoms or loss of function
- Imaging that suggests significant osteoarthritis, rotator cuff tear, meniscal tear, spinal stenosis, or other surgical pathology
- Need to evaluate whether surgery or injections/advanced non-surgical care is indicated
What to ask the PCP before referral:
- Will an ortho consult change management now, or should we try more conservative measures first (PT, injections, meds)?
