I can help — I’ll (A) summarize key things to discuss with your PCP about atrial fibrillation (AF) and (B) outline when an orthopedic referral makes sense given your age and vascular history. If you’d like, I can then draft or send an email to Dr. David Nguyen at Central Austin Primary Care with these questions.

A. Questions and points to raise about atrial fibrillation management
- Clarify diagnosis and rhythm status
  - Do you have a formal diagnosis of AF (paroxysmal, persistent, or permanent)? If so, when was it diagnosed and what tests have been done (EKG, Holter, echo)?
- Stroke prevention (anticoagulation)
  - Because you have a prior stroke, anticoagulation is usually strongly considered (prior stroke/TIA carries high risk). Ask whether you should start or continue an anticoagulant and which agent (DOAC vs warfarin) is preferable given your other medications and kidney function.
  - Ask them to calculate your CHA2DS2‑VASc and HAS‑BLED scores and explain bleeding risk and monitoring plan.
- Rate vs rhythm control
  - Ask if rate control (beta‑blocker, calcium channel blocker) or rhythm control (antiarrhythmic meds, cardioversion, ablation) is recommended for your situation and symptoms.
- Medication interactions and monitoring
  - Review interactions with your current meds (levothyroxine, lisinopril, atorvastatin) and any over‑the‑counter meds or supplements (e.g., NSAIDs, herbs).
  - Request baseline labs if appropriate (CBC, creatinine/estimated GFR, LFTs) for DOAC dosing and safety.
- Thyroid and other reversible contributors
  - Ask for TSH check — thyroid over- or under-replacement can trigger or worsen AF.
- Cardiac evaluation
  - Ask whether you need an echocardiogram (to check function/valves), ambulatory monitoring (Holter/loop recorder) if AF is suspected but intermittent, or referral to a cardiologist/electrophysiologist for consideration of ablation.
- Blood pressure and lipid control
  - Confirm BP goal and whether current lisinopril dose is adequate. Ensure atorvastatin is optimized given cardiovascular risk and stroke history.
- Practical questions
  - If anticoagulation is recommended: how to manage bleeding risk, what to do for procedures, and who will manage anticoagulation follow‑up.

B. How to decide about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- When to refer now
  - Significant pain limiting daily activities or sleep
  - Progressive functional loss (difficulty walking, stairs, dressing)
  - Failed conservative treatments (physical therapy, activity modification, injections, targeted meds)
  - Radiographic evidence of advanced joint disease on X‑ray or concern for deformity/fracture
- Considerations given your stroke history and age
  - If surgery is being considered, prior stroke increases perioperative cardiovascular risk — discuss pre‑op cardiac clearance and whether cardiology involvement is needed.
  - If you are (or will be) on anticoagulation, ask the orthopedist about perioperative anticoagulation planning.
- Practical approach
  - Start with PCP-directed conservative care (PT, injections, analgesics) and plain films if not already done. Ask PCP to refer to Northwest Orthopedics if symptoms are severe, progressive, or not responding to conservative care or if you want a surgical opinion.

Suggested clarifying questions for me before drafting/sending an email
- Do you already have a diagnosis of AF, or are you asking preemptively because of concern?
- Are you currently on any anticoagulant or antiplatelet medication?
- Which joint(s) are bothering you and what symptoms/treatments have you tried so far?
- Do you want me to draft an email to Dr. Nguyen summarizing these questions and requesting tests/referrals (cardiology and/or orthopedics)? If yes, should I send it or save it as a draft?

If you want, I can now:
- Draft an email to Dr. David Nguyen with the above questions and relevant medical details (I can include your meds, age, stroke history, last peak flow), or
- Send it immediately (I’ll need your confirmation to send).

Tell me which option you prefer and answer any of the clarifying questions you want included.

